  "id": "COMPOUND:25798 - PROTEIN:5839",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between 2-Nonyl-4-hydroxyquinoline N-oxide and U251, a glioblastoma cell line, through multiple pathways. The drug is implicated in targeting Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP, a key enzyme involved in cellular signaling and tumor progression. This enzyme is also a target of a complex compound, [(1S,2S,6R,7R,9R,10S,11S,12R,14S)-11-hydroxy-14-methyl-5-methylidene-4-oxospiro[3,13-dioxatetracyclo[8.4.0.02,6.012,14]tetradecane-9,2'-oxirane]-7-yl] (E)-4-hydroxy-2-methylbut-2-enoate, which is itself a target of U251. Additionally, the drug is linked to Hepatocyte, a cell type that is also a target of a complex compound that ultimately targets U251. These findings suggest that 2-Nonyl-4-hydroxyquinoline N-oxide may exert its effects through the inhibition of a signaling pathway involving Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP, which is a critical component of the tumor microenvironment. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between the drug and U251, though the exact molecular mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
